1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:02,680
Stretch a rubber band around an apple.

2
00:00:03,440 --> 00:00:07,180
You can slide it over the surface and
shrink it to a point, without tearing it.

3
00:00:08,070 --> 00:00:12,960
Around a doughnut, a band through the
hole cannot shrink. These are surfaces,

4
00:00:13,140 --> 00:00:14,960
so this picture lives in two dimensions.

5
00:00:15,790 --> 00:00:20,660
In nineteen oh four, Henri Poincaré
asked the same question one dimension up.

6
00:00:21,420 --> 00:00:23,500
Nearly a century passed
before it was answered.

7
00:00:24,290 --> 00:00:27,460
This film explains that answer:
the proof by Ricci flow,

8
00:00:27,460 --> 00:00:30,200
and what a computer proof
checker has now verified.

9
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:36,540
A three-dimensional manifold is a
space that looks, near every point,

10
00:00:36,920 --> 00:00:42,810
like ordinary space. We call it M. Closed
means that M is compact, with no edge.

11
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:45,580
The simplest example is the three-sphere.

12
00:00:46,220 --> 00:00:48,880
It is the set of points at
distance one from the origin,

13
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:50,610
in four-dimensional space.

14
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:52,280
We cannot draw it whole.

15
00:00:52,840 --> 00:00:56,320
But near each point it looks
like a piece of ordinary space,

16
00:00:56,860 --> 00:00:59,510
just as a small patch
of the earth looks flat.

17
00:01:00,140 --> 00:01:05,720
M is simply connected if it is connected
and every loop in M can be shrunk to

18
00:01:05,720 --> 00:01:07,370
a point, without leaving M.

19
00:01:07,980 --> 00:01:11,560
Poincaré's question,
in modern words: is every closed,

20
00:01:11,760 --> 00:01:15,060
simply connected three-manifold
homeomorphic to the three-sphere?

21
00:01:15,860 --> 00:01:20,310
Homeomorphic means matched point for
point, continuously in both directions.

22
00:01:20,840 --> 00:01:22,500
The loop test is essential.

23
00:01:23,480 --> 00:01:26,660
Poincaré himself found a space with
the same homology as the sphere,

24
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:30,510
yet with a loop that cannot shrink.
So it is not a sphere.

25
00:01:31,140 --> 00:01:34,240
In dimensions above three,
the question was settled first.

26
00:01:35,080 --> 00:01:39,860
Smale proved it for smooth manifolds from
dimension five up, in nineteen sixty-one.

27
00:01:39,860 --> 00:01:43,039
Freedman proved dimension four,
in nineteen eighty-two.

28
00:01:43,609 --> 00:01:46,020
Dimension three was the last case left.

29
00:01:48,033 --> 00:01:52,793
Richard Hamilton's idea was to give M a
shape, and let the shape improve itself.

30
00:01:52,953 --> 00:01:58,273
A metric, called g, measures lengths on M.
From it one computes curvature. R,

31
00:01:58,453 --> 00:02:01,343
the scalar curvature,
is one number at every point.

32
00:02:01,903 --> 00:02:04,933
In nineteen eighty-two,
Hamilton introduced the Ricci flow.

33
00:02:05,393 --> 00:02:07,093
The metric changes with time, t.

34
00:02:07,593 --> 00:02:10,413
Its rate of change is minus
two times the Ricci curvature,

35
00:02:10,973 --> 00:02:12,573
a curvature that depends on direction.

36
00:02:13,173 --> 00:02:17,023
Like heat spreading through a metal plate,
the flow tends to even out the curvature.

37
00:02:17,623 --> 00:02:20,143
A round sphere stays round and shrinks.

38
00:02:20,863 --> 00:02:25,783
Its radius squared drops by four in every
unit of time, until the sphere vanishes.

39
00:02:26,468 --> 00:02:29,818
Hamilton proved that if the Ricci
curvature is positive everywhere,

40
00:02:30,698 --> 00:02:35,558
the rescaled flow makes M round in
the limit. Then M is the three-sphere,

41
00:02:36,098 --> 00:02:38,748
or a quotient of it by a
finite group of rotations.

42
00:02:39,348 --> 00:02:44,178
In general, the flow can run into
singularities. Here a thin neck pinches,

43
00:02:44,178 --> 00:02:45,938
while the ends are still large.

44
00:02:46,578 --> 00:02:49,418
Each circle around this shape
stands for a whole two-sphere,

45
00:02:50,118 --> 00:02:52,218
so the picture shows a
three-dimensional space.

46
00:02:52,888 --> 00:02:57,898
Hamilton's plan was surgery: cut the neck,
cap the ends, and continue the flow.

47
00:02:58,698 --> 00:03:02,398
To make it work, one had to know
every shape a singularity can take.

48
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:06,780
Here is the plan of the proof, as a map.

49
00:03:07,540 --> 00:03:11,680
Five steps lead to a statement about the
flow. Four more turn it into topology.

50
00:03:12,060 --> 00:03:15,210
The first box, Ricci flow,
we have just seen.

51
00:03:15,840 --> 00:03:17,740
In two thousand two
and two thousand three,

52
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:22,360
Grisha Perelman posted three papers.
The first gives a kind of entropy,

53
00:03:22,820 --> 00:03:26,380
a quantity that never decreases
along the flow. For this,

54
00:03:26,760 --> 00:03:30,910
a companion function must evolve
backwards in time, by a heat equation.

55
00:03:31,410 --> 00:03:34,260
From it,
Perelman proved no local collapsing.

56
00:03:35,060 --> 00:03:37,120
Where the curvature is
controlled at some scale,

57
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:43,050
a ball of that size keeps a definite share
of its volume. That share is called kappa.

58
00:03:43,150 --> 00:03:46,890
Near a singularity,
one can zoom in and get a limit.

59
00:03:47,690 --> 00:03:52,470
The limits exist for all past time,
and Perelman calls them kappa solutions.

60
00:03:53,570 --> 00:03:57,660
For a fixed kappa, after rescaling,
they form a compact family.

61
00:03:58,530 --> 00:04:03,030
Every point of a kappa solution
sits in one of a few shapes. A neck,

62
00:04:03,670 --> 00:04:07,950
almost a round cylinder. A cap,
closing off a neck: a ball,

63
00:04:08,070 --> 00:04:10,030
or projective space with a ball removed.

64
00:04:10,030 --> 00:04:14,380
Or a whole closed piece of positive
curvature, often close to round.

65
00:04:15,240 --> 00:04:19,150
This is the canonical neighbourhood
theorem. Wherever the curvature is large,

66
00:04:19,630 --> 00:04:21,570
the flow looks like one of these shapes.

67
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:28,100
Now surgery can be done in a standard way.
Find a thin neck,

68
00:04:28,820 --> 00:04:32,720
cut it along its middle sphere,
and glue a standard cap onto each side.

69
00:04:33,820 --> 00:04:37,780
Pieces that are almost round are
set aside. Then the flow restarts.

70
00:04:38,370 --> 00:04:40,900
Each surgery removes a
definite amount of volume.

71
00:04:41,540 --> 00:04:44,200
So surgeries cannot pile
up in a finite time.

72
00:04:44,840 --> 00:04:48,260
Perelman proved that the canonical
neighbourhoods survive every surgery.

73
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:52,850
So the flow with surgery runs for
all time, from any starting shape.

74
00:04:53,570 --> 00:04:58,620
Topologically, cutting a neck undoes a
connected sum. If the neck separates M,

75
00:04:59,060 --> 00:05:01,330
then M falls into two simpler pieces.

76
00:05:02,010 --> 00:05:05,980
But a neck need not separate.
Picture a tube bent into a ring.

77
00:05:06,820 --> 00:05:08,360
Each circle stands for a two-sphere,

78
00:05:09,020 --> 00:05:11,600
so this is the product of
a two-sphere and a circle.

79
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:15,510
Cutting one neck leaves a single piece,
a three-sphere.

80
00:05:17,533 --> 00:05:21,353
One question remains:
does the flow with surgery ever stop?

81
00:05:21,773 --> 00:05:27,453
For a simply connected M, it does.
After a finite time, nothing is left.

82
00:05:28,233 --> 00:05:29,633
This is finite extinction.

83
00:05:30,223 --> 00:05:33,213
Perelman measures M by a width, called W.

84
00:05:34,133 --> 00:05:36,513
Take a family of loops
that sweeps across M.

85
00:05:37,353 --> 00:05:39,193
Fill each loop with a disc of least area.

86
00:05:39,993 --> 00:05:43,403
W is the smallest possible
value of the largest disc area.

87
00:05:43,883 --> 00:05:47,973
Under the flow,
d W by d t is at most minus two pi,

88
00:05:48,653 --> 00:05:50,753
minus one half of R min times W.

89
00:05:51,793 --> 00:05:54,683
Here R min is the smallest
scalar curvature at that time.

90
00:05:55,103 --> 00:05:57,693
And the scalar curvature
cannot become very negative.

91
00:05:58,513 --> 00:06:01,733
R min is at least minus three halves,
over t plus c,

92
00:06:02,253 --> 00:06:04,743
where c is a constant fixed
by the starting metric.

93
00:06:05,623 --> 00:06:07,013
Put the two together and integrate.

94
00:06:07,913 --> 00:06:12,053
The width is held below a curve that
reaches zero at a finite time, T star.

95
00:06:12,953 --> 00:06:16,573
A width cannot be negative,
so by T star nothing is left.

96
00:06:17,258 --> 00:06:20,018
This deadline holds for
every piece at once.

97
00:06:20,838 --> 00:06:23,238
Trace any surviving piece
back through the surgeries.

98
00:06:23,938 --> 00:06:28,218
Its ancestors lead to M at the start,
so its width starts at the same value,

99
00:06:28,618 --> 00:06:33,608
W zero. Surgery never raises the width.
One deadline covers them all.

100
00:06:34,208 --> 00:06:39,098
Colding and Minicozzi gave a second proof,
sweeping with spheres instead of loops.

101
00:06:39,678 --> 00:06:42,658
Their width falls with
four pi in place of two pi.

102
00:06:43,378 --> 00:06:45,358
Each route keeps its own constants.

103
00:06:45,848 --> 00:06:47,218
Now read the history backwards.

104
00:06:48,198 --> 00:06:51,738
Every piece that vanished was built
from the three-sphere, its quotients,

105
00:06:52,158 --> 00:06:54,038
and the product of a
two-sphere and a circle.

106
00:06:54,618 --> 00:06:56,758
So M is a connected sum of such pieces.

107
00:06:57,278 --> 00:07:00,978
M is simply connected, so every
piece must be simply connected too.

108
00:07:01,698 --> 00:07:06,458
Only the three-sphere qualifies. So M is
the three-sphere, as a smooth manifold.

109
00:07:06,958 --> 00:07:10,798
If M comes without a smooth structure,
Moise's theorem supplies one.

110
00:07:10,938 --> 00:07:14,088
So the conjecture holds,
exactly as Poincaré asked it.

111
00:07:16,100 --> 00:07:19,440
Perelman never published his
papers in a journal. Instead,

112
00:07:20,100 --> 00:07:23,700
teams of mathematicians checked
them in full. By two thousand six,

113
00:07:24,340 --> 00:07:29,200
Kleiner and Lott, Morgan and Tian, and
Cao and Zhu had posted complete accounts.

114
00:07:29,670 --> 00:07:32,840
The proof is accepted.
In two thousand ten,

115
00:07:33,180 --> 00:07:36,960
the Clay Mathematics Institute
awarded Perelman its Millennium Prize,

116
00:07:37,580 --> 00:07:39,930
crediting these accounts
and years of scrutiny.

117
00:07:40,935 --> 00:07:44,395
That is a judgement made by people.
What follows is different:

118
00:07:44,395 --> 00:07:47,075
a machine check of a formal proof.

119
00:07:49,100 --> 00:07:53,020
In twenty twenty-six,
Ziyang Qin, Yuan Liao,

120
00:07:53,580 --> 00:07:58,200
Ayush Khaitan and Bennett Chow formalized
the proof in Lean, a proof assistant.

121
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:02,610
Lean's kernel checks every step of a
proof against the rules of its logic.

122
00:08:03,680 --> 00:08:07,220
The formal proof follows Hamilton
and Perelman, through Ricci flow,

123
00:08:08,100 --> 00:08:11,540
surgery and finite extinction,
and it includes Moise's theorem.

124
00:08:11,920 --> 00:08:16,380
So every box of the plan has a
formal counterpart, proved in Lean.

125
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:19,230
A blue seal marks a box proved in Lean.

126
00:08:20,170 --> 00:08:25,540
On MathDB, the conjecture is stated
in Lean. Here it is, clause by clause.

127
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:30,700
For every space M, with a topology, in
which two points can always be kept apart,

128
00:08:31,180 --> 00:08:34,610
with charts that make M look like
three-dimensional space near every point,

129
00:08:35,180 --> 00:08:38,180
if M is homotopy equivalent
to the three-sphere, that is,

130
00:08:38,500 --> 00:08:42,170
there are maps both ways whose composites
can be deformed to the identity,

131
00:08:42,690 --> 00:08:45,140
then M is homeomorphic
to the three-sphere.

132
00:08:46,190 --> 00:08:50,340
Compactness is not assumed. The proof
derives it, and simple connectivity,

133
00:08:50,340 --> 00:08:54,930
from the homotopy equivalence. Then it
applies the theorem we have followed.

134
00:08:55,480 --> 00:09:00,540
The check uses only Lean's three standard
axioms: propositional extensionality,

135
00:09:00,920 --> 00:09:06,090
the axiom of choice, and the soundness
of quotients. No step is left unfinished.

136
00:09:06,640 --> 00:09:10,600
What does the check not certify?
First, Perelman's papers.

137
00:09:11,180 --> 00:09:14,320
The kernel checks the formal proof,
not any written text.

138
00:09:14,800 --> 00:09:19,440
Second, the library's own definitions,
of Ricci flow, surgery and width.

139
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:24,130
Its authors state that they checked them
by hand. We know of no independent review.

140
00:09:24,680 --> 00:09:29,200
So to trust this result, you trust three
things. The Lean kernel and its tools.

141
00:09:29,760 --> 00:09:32,140
That the words of the final
statement mean what we said.

142
00:09:32,680 --> 00:09:34,900
And that this statement
is Poincaré's question.

143
00:09:35,420 --> 00:09:38,380
In short:
mathematicians accept Perelman's proof,

144
00:09:38,760 --> 00:09:42,080
and the formal statement now has
a complete, machine-checked proof.

145
00:09:44,100 --> 00:09:48,340
One relative of the question is
still open. In dimension four,

146
00:09:48,800 --> 00:09:53,420
is every smooth homotopy four-sphere the
standard sphere, as a smooth manifold?

147
00:09:54,340 --> 00:09:55,340
Nobody knows.

